<document lang="en" type="presentation" theme-accent="blue">
  <title>Delta decks</title>

  <!-- M7 item 32 only: `type="presentation"` activates the deck theme (full-bleed,
       projector-scale type) and the `data-type` hook on <html>. The <slide> element
       and per-slide chrome arrive in item 33; viewport paging + keyboard navigation
       in item 36. Until then a deck is a tall, full-bleed scroll of its sections. -->

  <section id="intro">
    <eyebrow>A test deck</eyebrow>
    <title>Presentations in Delta</title>
    <intro>
      The same strict-XML source, compiled to a single offline HTML file — now at
      projector scale. Inline math like $e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$ renders as usual.
    </intro>
  </section>

  <section id="math">
    <title>Math still works</title>
    Display math is centered and large:
    <equation id="eq:euler">\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\,dx = \sqrt{\pi}</equation>

    <theorem id="thm:pyth">
      <title>Pythagoras</title>
      For a right triangle with legs $a$, $b$ and hypotenuse $c$,
      <equation id="eq:pyth">a^2 + b^2 = c^2</equation>
    </theorem>
  </section>

  <section id="content">
    <title>Ordinary chrome at deck scale</title>
    Lists, emphasis, and references all carry over. We can point back at
    <ref to="thm:pyth"/> from anywhere.
    <list marker="number">
      <item>One idea per point.</item>
      <item>Built from the same tags you already know.</item>
      <item>Still a single, fully offline file.</item>
    </list>
  </section>
</document>
